[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a standard differential geometry fact. Let me think through it.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mA compact Riemannian manifold (M,g) is a smooth compact manifold with a [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mRiemannian metric g. The metric g is smooth (by definition of Riemannian [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmanifold). The Ricci curvature Ric is a (0,2)-tensor obtained by contracting the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mRiemann curvature tensor, which is built from the metric and its derivatives (up[0m[38;2;124;124;124mto second order) via Christoffel symbols.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince M is compact and g is smooth, all the components of the metric in any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mlocal coordinate chart are smooth functions. The Christoffel symbols involve [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfirst derivatives of g, and the Riemann tensor involves first derivatives of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mChristoffel symbols (second derivatives of g). The Ricci tensor is a contraction[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof the Riemann tensor.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mKey argument: M is compact, so we can cover it with finitely many coordinate [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcharts. In each chart, the components of Ric are smooth functions (since g is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msmooth). A smooth function on a compact set (the closure of a coordinate chart [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mintersected with M, or more precisely on the compact coordinate patch) is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbounded. Since there are finitely many charts, the Ricci curvature is bounded [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mglobally.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore carefully: The Ricci tensor is a smooth (0,2)-tensor field on M because the[0m[38;2;124;124;124mmetric is smooth. A continuous function on a compact topological space is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mbounded. The components of Ric in any local frame are continuous (in fact [0m
[38;2;124;124;124msmooth) functions. [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, the cleanest argument: Ric is a smooth tensor field on M. As a smooth [0m[38;2;124;124;124msection of a tensor bundle over compact M, its norm |Ric| (with respect to any [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mauxiliary metric, e.g., g itself) is a continuous function on compact M, hence [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mattains a maximum, hence is bounded. Therefore Ric is bounded.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe answer is yes, the Ricci curvature must be bounded.[0m

